1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:13,000
Oakdene House is a special place. An oasis of calm, compassion and hope in an often chaotic world.

2
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,000
It's a place where people actually can come and feel not judged at all.

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Here, free counselling and support services are offered to people affected by addiction.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Its support Anne Marie Durnan knows first hand can change lives.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Unfortunately after a year of marriage my husband left

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
and it just happened to be the same timing as pokies arriving in Victoria.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
That was the beginning of the end.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
After seeking help for her gambling addiction, Anne Marie has now been in recovery for 14 years.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Today she's helping others take those same first steps.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
They say connection is the opposite of addiction and that's priceless.

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Our job is to treat everyone with dignity, courtesy and respect.

12
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Anthony Sobb is founding chairman of Oakdene House and long time CEO of nearby Fairfield RSL.

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
We have a phrase here, we do things differently at Fairfield RSL.

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,000
I come from lived experience in relation to addiction, over four generations in my family.

15
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:15,000
So you've seen it?

16
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Yeah I have.

17
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,000
The idea for Oakdene House was born after Anthony visited the International Problem Gambling Centre in Las Vegas

18
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
and realised there was more his own club could do.

19
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Very much like alcohol there's a percentage of the population that really has adverse consequences to their gambling.

20
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Professor Bo Bernhard from the University of Nevada is one of the world's leading researchers in gambling harm and responsible gaming.

21
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Over the last quarter century the science on treatment has really evolved

22
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
and treatment is now really really good.

23
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Anthony Sobb is the only gaming executive I've ever had in 25 years of teaching

24
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,000
who asked to go to the Problem Gambling Centre and sit with people who are going through treatment

25
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
and listen to their stories of pain.

26
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
One thing is for sure you can't beat this stuff on your own.

27
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,000
According to lead counsellor Mark Henson, who was in recovery himself,

28
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,000
the greatest victories experienced at Oakdene House aren't simply about what people give up,

29
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
but what they gain.

30
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
It's about rebuilding the families, getting back together.

31
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Now they've got children, now they're getting on with their lives.

32
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
There should be an Oakdene House in every state in Australia.

33
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
But board member Tom Simpson, who for many years also experienced addiction,

34
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,000
the success of Oakdene House comes down to its people.

35
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:44,000
When you are an addict, what's it like coming to a place like Oakdene House?

36
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,000
I don't know Tim, it's hard to explain, but if I've never had cancer,

37
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
it's no good when they're going to a cancer clinic and saying I know how you feel.

38
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Because I really don't.

39
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
But when someone that's been there and tells you about it,

40
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,000
you just know, they know what we're talking about, they're genuine.

41
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Now, through the work of Oakdene Foundation, that support extends even further,

42
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
helping people facing food insecurity, financial hardship and domestic violence

43
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
through a nearby community kitchen, laundrette and ladies boutique.

44
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
This is a very unique way of a club supporting its community.

45
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
What do you say to critics?

46
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
You don't do anything and you're apathetic.

47
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,000
You do do something and you shouldn't be involved in that.

48
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
So I just made a conscious decision to say that I was going to do something about it.

49
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:37,000
For Anne Marie Durnan,

50
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,000
I think to myself, what would have happened if this was around back then?

51
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,000
The impact of the work done at Oakdene House every day isn't measured in appointments,

52
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,000
programs or services, but in the hope of helping someone reclaim their life.

53
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
What's it like seeing someone that has recovered?

54
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,000
I'd probably cry. The relief someone gets,

55
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,000
it's like the chains have been cut and they get to be the best version of themselves again.

